    thinking: "**Verifying Metadata Configuration**\n\nOkay, I\'ve got the metadata.json structure set. I\'ve added the name as \"Hemi Toolkit\" and crafted a description: \"A secure, offline-first client-side productivity toolkit featuring robust PDF utilities, image editors, developer formatting, cryptographic keys, and more.\" Now, let\'s just make sure everything is in order.\n\n\n**Considering React Structure**\n\nI\'m now determining the optimal framework. While I initially considered a plain HTML/CSS/JS setup for a GitHub Pages deployment as requested, the existing project dependencies indicate React/TypeScript is already the intended structure, and that\'s likely the best path forward given it supports everything the user requested. I see an existing foundation for `src/App.tsx`, `src/index.css`, `src/main.tsx` and can easily divide things into components.\n\n\n**Analyzing Deployment Approach**\n\nI\'m now weighing the merits of a modular React application against a vanilla HTML/CSS/JS (or TS) setup bundled with Vite, given the user\'s explicit preference for separate files and GitHub Pages deployment. It seems Vite\'s multi-page or static site bundling, coupled with TypeScript support, aligns perfectly with the user\'s requirements. This includes using index.html, with linked CSS and JS files, which will be much easier to troubleshoot.\n\n\n**Confirming Implementation Details**\n\nOkay, so I\'ve confirmed that a modular TypeScript setup within Vite perfectly fits the bill. The plan is to create clean TypeScript modules that Vite efficiently compiles into optimized JavaScript chunks, alongside bundled CSS. This aligns perfectly with the user\'s preference for separate HTML, CSS, and script files. We\'ll have a streamlined `/index.html`, `/src/index.css` for the stylesheet (including Tailwind CSS), and `/src/main.ts` as the main entry point, initializing the application. The result will be beautiful, clean, and mathematically elegant.\n\n\n**Organizing Toolkit Modules**\n\nI\'m now architecting the modular structure. I\'ll create an application router, handle theme changes, and bootstrap tools. Key type definitions go in `/src/types.ts`. `/src/tools/` houses separate TypeScript files for each tool: `htmlToPdf.ts`, `pdfMerge.ts`, `imageCompress.ts`, `pdfSplit.ts`, `passwordGen.ts`, `qrGen.ts`, `jsonFmt.ts`, `pdfRotate.ts`, `pdfWatermark.ts`, `pdfMeta.ts`, `pdfNumbers.ts`, `pdfProtect.ts`, and `colors.ts`. This modular approach is brilliant for maintainability and avoiding token limits. I\'m also checking for necessary npm packages.\n\n\n**Evaluating Package Installations**\n\nI\'m now determining the deployment method for external libraries. The existing code uses CDN scripts, but I\'m leaning toward installing packages via `npm` and bundling them with Vite for offline functionality and a self-contained application. This ensures robustness against potential CDN issues. Specifically, I\'m verifying the presence of libraries such as `pdf-lib`, `jspdf`, `canvas-confetti`, and others to optimize the import method.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Library Deployment**\n\nI\'m now determining how to handle external libraries, and it looks like using a script loader will allow for cleaner imports. Specifically, I\'ve confirmed that dynamic loading via the script tag and CDN-based approach for `pdf.js` and `pdf-lib` is the best method to avoid complex configurations. I am now evaluating the implementation.\n\n\n\n**Debugging Password Generator**\n\nI\'ve got the password generator bugs to fix! The user\'s script references missing checkboxes. I need to add these to the UI HTML. I\'ll need checkboxes for upper case, numbers, and special symbols, each with appropriate `id` attributes. This calls for a redesign of the UI, with sliders and toggles for user satisfaction.\n\n\n**Adjusting Font Configuration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the fonts into the Tailwind configuration. I\'m focusing on adding `JetBrains Mono` as a font option within Tailwind to improve the code-viewing experience. I\'m also ensuring the base path in `vite.config.ts` will support asset resolution within AI Studio and on GitHub Pages! With `base: \'./\'` asset paths should resolve regardless of location.\n\n\n**Confirming Cross-origin Font Loading**\n\nI\'m now verifying the crossorigin attribute in the iframe\'s HTML structure for printing to avoid font loading errors, while focusing on supporting Google Fonts in the print preview. I must ensure the correct font styles are used, which may require specific CSS declarations, as well as confirm the iframe\'s content with its fonts.\n\n\n\n**Refining Vite Configuration**\n\nOkay, I\'ve confirmed that the existing React plugin in Vite is unnecessary for the static TypeScript SPA I\'m building, but it doesn\'t hurt to have it. I\'ve switched the `index.html` to point to `/src/main.ts`, and checked the linter and compiler, which both work. This aligns perfectly with the desired separation of HTML, CSS, and TS files.\n\n\n**Structuring Project Modules**\n\nI\'m now outlining a file structure for all toolkit modules. I\'ll include `/src/types.ts` for shared interfaces and `/src/utils.ts` for utility functions. I\'ve also planned separate files for each tool, from HTML to PDF to QR code generation, image conversions, and security tools. This modular organization will ensure maintainability.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 21
      nanos: 239923810
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
