  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Discovering Shared Patterns**\n\nI\'ve noticed a recurring pattern: most of these tools employ a standard `#drop-zone` and `#file-input` setup for file handling. From pdf-merge to img-to-pdf, the same core structure seems prevalent. I\'m starting to think this shared architecture could lead to a more modular and efficient design going forward.\n\n\n**Implementing Global Event Handlers**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a grander design concept! Since many tools (convert, sign-pdf, rotate-pdf...) share the `#drop-zone` and `#file-input` setup, I\'m thinking of creating a global event listener in `src/main.ts`. I believe that with a MutationObserver, event delegate, or `router.openTool` check, I can automatically bind drag-and-drop actions across all tools, ensuring consistent behavior for single and multi-file uploads. This modular design will be far cleaner than past approaches.\n\n\n**Refining Drop-Zone Logic**\n\nI\'ve been working on refining the drag-and-drop logic. I\'m excited about this; when a file is dragged over the body or `#drop-zone`, I\'m going to listen to container `dragover`, `dragleave`, and `drop` events. For file dropping, I\'ll be assigning `ev.dataTransfer.files` to the `#file-input` element\'s `files` property via a `DataTransfer` object and dispatching a `change` event. This will trigger the file input listener of *any* active tool!\n\n\n**Integrating Global File Handler**\n\nI\'m now integrating the global file handler. It seems straightforward now that `MutationObserver` is fully supported! The plan is to place the global watcher inside `src/main.ts`, running after `router.openTool()` completes. Specifically, after `handler.init()`, I\'ll call a `setupDropZoneListeners()` utility function. This design will standardize the drop-zone events across tools. I\'ve started the implementation and it\'s looking promising so far.\n\n\n**Defining Drop-Zone Functions**\n\nNow, I\'m focusing on the core drop-zone event handlers. I\'m writing `ondragover`, `ondragleave`, and `ondrop` functions to standardize behavior. I\'m preventing default browser actions, managing \'drag-over-active\' classes, and crucially, assigning dropped files to the hidden file input. I\'m also triggering a \'change\' event on the input, so any active tool reacts. I am catching and reporting any potential errors.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will view lines 160 to 195 of `src/main.ts` to locate where `handler.init()` runs so we can execute `setupDropZoneListeners()` right after."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 6
      nanos: 484996601
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
